---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfWordCounter from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfWordCounter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-word-counter">
  <PdfWordCounter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Why counting words in a PDF is harder than it sounds</h2>
      <p>
        A word processor knows what a word is, because you typed it. A PDF does not. It stores
        positioned runs of glyphs (&ldquo;draw these three characters at x=112.4, y=703.8 in this
        font at this size&rdquo;), and one visual word is often split across several runs
        because of kerning, ligatures or a change of font.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recovering words means reading every glyph run on a page, measuring the horizontal gap
        between consecutive runs, and deciding whether that gap is a space or just kerning. This
        tool uses the run height as the yardstick: a gap wider than about 16% of the line height
        becomes a space, and a vertical jump of more than half a line height becomes a line break.
        It is the same heuristic a PDF reader uses when you select text with the mouse.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Expect a small disagreement with Word</h2>
      <p>
        Counts from a PDF and counts from the source document will differ by a percent or two, in
        both directions, for reasons that are inherent rather than fixable:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Headers, footers and page numbers are text on the page and get counted.</li>
        <li>A word hyphenated across a line break may be counted as two.</li>
        <li>Text inside images (a chart label, a scanned stamp) is not counted.</li>
        <li>Footnote markers, table cells and captions all count as words here.</li>
        <li>Soft hyphens, ligatures and non-breaking spaces are normalised differently by different writers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Use the figure for quoting, planning and pricing. For a legally binding count (a thesis
        limit, a court filing), count in the source document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Counting CJK</h2>
      <p>
        Chinese, Japanese and Korean do not separate words with spaces, so whitespace counting
        returns near-zero for a document that plainly has thousands of words in it. The convention
        in those languages, and in translation pricing for them, is to count characters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every kana, CJK ideograph, compatibility ideograph, half-width katakana and Hangul syllable
        is counted as one unit. Latin text in the same document is still counted by whitespace, so a
        bilingual document totals sensibly rather than being wrong in one direction or the other.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Standard pages and per-word rates</h2>
      <p>
        Translation and editing are priced by the word almost everywhere, but the unit of a
        &ldquo;page&rdquo; varies by market:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Market</th>
            <th scope="col">Standard page</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>UK / US / general</td><td>250 words</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Germany (Normzeile)</td><td>55 characters per line, 30 lines: 1,650 characters</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Poland (strona rozliczeniowa)</td><td>1,800 characters including spaces</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Italy (cartella)</td><td>1,500 characters including spaces</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Russia</td><td>1,800 characters including spaces</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The rate tab shows both the 250-word and the 1,800-character page counts alongside the fee
        at your own per-word rate, with the arithmetic written out so you can paste it straight into
        a quote.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reading and speaking time</h2>
      <p>
        The default of 238 words per minute is the mean silent reading rate for adults reading
        English non-fiction, from Brysbaert&rsquo;s 2019 meta-analysis of 190 studies covering
        nearly eighteen thousand participants. It is a mean, not a target: the same analysis puts
        the range for ordinary adults roughly between 175 and 300.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reading aloud is much slower. The 130 words per minute used for the spoken estimate is a
        comfortable presentation pace. Audiobook narration is about 150, and anything above 180
        starts to sound rushed. If you are timing a conference talk, use the spoken figure and add
        time for slides.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Scanned PDFs, honestly</h2>
      <p>
        If the tool reports almost no words, the document has no text layer: it is a photograph of a
        page. To read it needs optical character recognition, which needs a trained model of several
        megabytes from a server. This site does not fetch such models, so it has no OCR. The tool
        tells you so, and does not return zero and let you think the file is empty.
      </p>
      <p>
        The quickest way to confirm it yourself: open the PDF in any reader and try to select a word
        with the mouse. If nothing highlights, there is no text there to count.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
